  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 230 PM EST Sunday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry and seasonably cool through the middle of \nthe week. \n\nBeautiful fall weather is ongoing and will continue for the next \nseveral days. Latest sfc analysis indicates high pressure centered \nover the south-central CONUS expanding eastward to cover the \nSoutheast. It is being held back from building fully into the Mid-\nAtlantic by low pressure systems both north of the Great Lakes and \nnear Nova Scotia. Aloft, A trough is in place over the Great Lakes \nand New England placing the local area under relatively weak NW \nflow. Not expecting temps to get quite so chilly tonight since the \npositioning of the high will allow for a light SW breeze thus \nlimiting radiational cooling. Looking at lows in the low to mid 40s. \nTomorrow, a dry cold front will push south and NW winds will become \nbreezy during the afternoon behind it. While highs will be able to \nget up into the mid 60s, gusts of 15 to 25 mph will keep it feeling \nmore like the upper 50s. Winds likely diminish in time for efficient \nradiational cooling Monday night leading to lows in the 30s for most \nplaces, low 40s immediately near the coast. High pressure slides \neast, the center of it staying to the south, Tuesday and Wednesday. \nFlow aloft becomes more zonal. As a result, temps become a little \nmilder with highs around 70 both days. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A strong cold front brings chances for rain \nThursday-Friday, though models have trended drier. Below Average \ntemperatures follow behind the front. \n\nGuidance is pretty much all on the same page in the 12z runs \nsignaling a big pattern shift at the end of the week. Everything \nindicates an anomalous 500mb trough developing across the eastern \nhalf of the country Thursday into Friday. This would mean that much \ncooler, below average temperatures are on the way for Friday and the \nweekend. NBM puts highs in the low-mid 50s through Sunday, but would \nnot be surprised to see some spots struggle to leave the 40s on \nSaturday. Overnight lows look to be around freezing each night away \nfrom the immediate coast. Jury is still out on how much rain we can \nget out of this system. Ensembles did trend a tad lower with respect \nto QPF. At this point it seems like even 0.5\" is unlikely with the \nwettest of the ensembles (ECMWF Ens) only show 20-40% probs for QPF \n>0.5\". The GEFS and GEPS barely show any probs in this category. \nProbs for >0.1\" jump up to 40-70%. Areas north of I-64 have the best \nshot at appreciable rainfall Thursday evening through Friday.",
